                <pb facs="0565" n="485" sameAs="frus:frus1902/pg485"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Naphen to Mr.
                            Hay.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >House of Representatives</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1902-01-16"><hi rendition="#italic">January 16,
                        1902</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Sir</hi>: The inclosed letter this day
                    received from the brother of James L. Molloy, who is now detained at Bermuda as
                    a prisoner of war. From this you will see that Molloy was forced to enter the
                    Boer army. I feel that under the circumstances, especially in view of the fact
                    that he is sick, he is entitled to be paroled.</p>
                <p>Very respectfully,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">H. F. Naphen.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
